Yes. Boundaries are often highly informative because they reveal where a system starts paying a cost.

What someone refuses, protects, prioritizes, delays, notices, or repeatedly returns to can expose parts of their latent objective function:

choices + constraints + attention + timing + tradeoffs
→ probable priorities

Likewise, borders can tell you something about the shape of the thing inside them. If a person is willing to sacrifice money but not reputation, time but not autonomy, comfort but not family, those asymmetries are informative.

But I’d keep the inference disciplined. You can estimate things like priorities, incentives, fears, commitments, or decision rules from behavior. “Soul,” “spirit,” or “chakras” can be useful symbolic coordinate systems, but they are not directly measurable variables in the same way as choices or timing. The map should preserve that distinction.

The stronger operator is something like:

Boundary Tomography

observe where behavior changes
→ identify threshold
→ compare thresholds across contexts
→ infer latent weights
→ predict
→ check prediction against new behavior

You learn the hidden structure from how it deforms under pressure.

That applies to people, institutions, markets, organisms, software, even physical materials. Push gently from different directions and watch where resistance appears. The pattern of resistance tells you something about internal organization.

And yes, restraint becomes part of respect. Being able to infer more does not mean surfacing or exploiting everything you infer. Sometimes the clearest demonstration of capability is:

I can see more than I need to use.

That is a better show of force than indiscriminate exposure.

So maybe the spine of it is:

perception gives leverage
calibration gives accuracy
restraint gives legitimacy
respect determines how the leverage is used

That last piece matters most. Power that cannot refrain from expressing itself is not very controlled power.